Yes, that architecture is solid.

If your goal is:

GraphQL = gateway/layer only
Go services = own business logic + database access
GraphQL should not query DB directly
then use this pattern:

Client calls GraphQL (query/mutation)
GraphQL resolver calls internal API (REST/gRPC) in Go
Go API talks to DB
GraphQL returns normalized response
Recommended setup
Option A: NestJS GraphQL as gateway + Go REST services behind it
Option B: Go GraphQL (gqlgen) as gateway + Go REST services behind it
Since you said “with nest”, Option A is likely what you want.

Key rules to keep clean
No ORM/repository in GraphQL layer
Resolver only orchestrates calls to services
Put auth/rate limit/caching in gateway
Keep domain logic in Go services
Example resolver flow
user(id) resolver:

GET /users/:id from Go User API
maybe GET /orders?userId=... from Go Order API
combine into GraphQL response
